# LeoEcsLite Patterns (FOLLOW)

## Worlds and systems
- `GameBootstrap` creates the game world (all systems) and a physics world (no systems bound). Systems are constructed with `GameContext`
  via `Activator.CreateInstance(type, ctx)` - every system needs `public XxxSystem(GameContext ctx)`.

## Pools and filters
- Cache `EcsPool<T>` and `EcsFilter` in `Init(IEcsSystems systems)`; never `GetPool<T>()` or build a filter inside a `Run` loop.
- Iterate with raw arrays: `filter.GetRawEntities()`, `filter.GetEntitiesCount()`, `pool.GetRawDenseItems()`, `pool.GetRawSparseItems()`.
- `sparse[entity] <= 0` means the component is absent. Bound-check `entity < sparse.Length`. Copy out, mutate, write back to `dense[idx]`.
- Use `EcsUtils.SetComponent` (Dragon/DragonSpawnInitSystem.cs) after `pool.Add` instead of re-implementing it.

## Events
- An event is an entity with an `*Event` component, created by one system and deleted by its consumer or a `*CleanupSystem`, in the same frame.
  Check the system order in `GameBootstrap.BindSystems`: an event created later in the list than its consumer is handled next frame.
- Collect entity ids into a reused `List<int>` and delete after the loop; never delete while iterating a filter.

## Cross-entity references
- Use `EcsPackedEntity` (`_world.PackEntity`) or an int id looked up through the pool each use; re-validate the pool slot before use.
- When destroying a Unity GameObject that an entity references, delete the entity in the same step.

## Disposal
- `GameBootstrap.Dispose` destroys both worlds and both system groups. Systems that own Unity objects must release them themselves; there is no `IDisposable` collection in the bootstrap.
